// ==========================================================================
// data/planes/cessna172/datcom.jsonc for the Cessna 172 source analysis, generated -- do not hand-edit.
//
// 1. SOLVER, MESH, FLIGHT CONDITION
//    Solver: Digital DATCOM.  TWO solvers, because each answers a different
//    question: (a) the Fortran binary run by aid.datcom_run.run_datcom on a card
//    file written into a TemporaryDirectory -- binary at
//    Matlab/fsroot/code/DATCOM/datcom via aid.paths.datcom_wrapper -- supplies
//    the longitudinal static set (CLA, CMA, CL and CM rows) and the two
//    intercepts; (b) aid.handbook_pass.apply_handbook supplies what the Fortran
//    table does not carry: the parasite drag build-up (aid.drag), the vertical-
//    tail geometry DATCOM Section 7 needs, and the control derivatives
//    (aid.handbook_controls).  There is no mesh: DATCOM is the handbook method.
//    Evaluated at alpha = 4 deg, the middle entry of AERO.ALSCHD, as aid/tornado_io.py::_build_state picks it.
//    Flight condition from the source AERO: Mach 0.03, altitude 0.0 ft, betha 0; the Fortran's own
//    for006 header echoes `$FLTCON ... ALT=0.00, MACH=0.030`, so the Fortran half of
//    this file runs at the source condition.
//    AID hands its drivers `mach * atm['a'] / 3.28084` = 10.207345 and labels it ft/s, and the Fortran
//    works its reference geometry in feet, so the solver speed is 10.2073 ft/s = 3.1112 m/s
//    in this model's frame.  The same expression evaluated in SI is the PHYSICAL speed 10.2073 m/s at a
//    sound speed of 1116.2889 ft/s -- and that is the speed flow5's SI deck used, which is
//    why avl.jsonc and flow5.jsonc record different source speeds for the same Mach 0.03.  Both readings are
//    in provenance.flight_condition and neither is reconciled away.
//    The trimmed cruise speed the model actually flies at is 23.1141 m/s.
//
// 2. THE SOURCE PLANFORM IS A CESSNA 172 AT ROUGHLY 1/3 LINEAR SCALE
//    span 12 ft against a real 36.1 ft, chord 2 ft against 4.89 ft, area 24 ft^2
//    against 174 ft^2.  Wing loading is therefore 6.67 lbf/ft^2 where a real C172 is
//    13.2.  Nothing in this file may be compared with a full-scale figure.
//
// 3. MASS
//    mass_kg = 72.574 is derived from the source's own AERO.WT = 5 slugs:
//    floor(AERO.WT * 32.0 ft/s^2 / 2.2046226 lbf/kg * 1000)/1000 = 72.574 kg, truncated from
//    72.5747799 kg, i.e. 160 lbf.  A slug is a mass unit, so the
//    source's WT converts instead as AERO.WT * SLUG_TO_KG = 72.96951 kg; the plan's figure assumes g = 32.0 ft/s^2, which
//    appears nowhere in the source (its own G_FT_S2 is 32.174), so the plan is 0.54 % low and
//    everything derived from the weight is too.
//    This is NOT a geometrically similar 1/3 scale C172, which would weigh about 53.5 kg, so no
//    scaling of a published full-scale number is self-consistent and none was attempted.
//    ixx, iyy, izz come from radius-of-gyration rules on THIS mass and THIS span (see below);
//    ixz = 0 and he = 0.  All four Cessna files derive mass and inertia identically from the same
//    geometry, so they are consistent with each other and with tornado.jsonc.
//    xcg and xcg_ref are BOTH 0.0 here, where tornado.jsonc carries -0.896112 for both, and that
//    difference is deliberate rather than an oversight: these three solvers report about the CG
//    itself (verified in item 5), so the CG's offset FROM THE MOMENT REFERENCE is zero, while for
//    Tornado it is the 2.94 ft station shift.  The schema's own `Cm + Cz*(xcgref - xcg)` term is
//    identically zero either way, because xcg == xcg_ref on every reachable path.
//
// 4. UNIT CONVERSIONS APPLIED, AND THE ONES NOT TRUSTED
//    length: ft x 0.3048 = 0.3048 m; angle: degrees x 57.29577951308232 (= DEG_TO_RAD) per radian.
//    THE FORTRAN'S OWN STABILITY TABLE IS PER DEGREE.  Measured against its own
//    cl column, `cla` equals the secant dCL/dalpha in per degree to three
//    decimals (0.10030/deg against a centred secant of
//    0.10038/deg over +/-4 deg).  Every derivative read out of it is multiplied by
//    57.29577951308232 by this converter.
//    AID's HELPBOOK SLOPES ARE NOT TRUSTED EITHER, and three separate defects on
//    this path were corrected rather than inherited:
//      (1) aid.stability._per_deg, aid.handbook_controls._per_deg and aid.handbook_pass._per_rad are all
//          `if a > 1: convert else pass`.  This planform's slopes are
//          0.076-0.084 /deg, so every one of them is a NO-OP and the number
//          that needs a per-radian value is handed the per-degree one.
//          Measured: aid's own longitudinal_dynamic CLq = 0.103851 where 57.2958x it is
//          5.950221, and aid's own lateral_dynamic Clp = -0.013938 against
//          -0.798610.  The DATCOM Section 7 formulae are therefore
//          recomputed in this converter with the factor applied by hand, and the
//          uncorrected numbers are kept in provenance.handbook_cross_check.
//      (2) aid/lateral.py:570 ends its tail lift-slope formula with `vt_a * pi/180`,
//          which is the wrong conversion in the wrong direction.  The formula's
//          `k` comes from aid/lateral.py:565, which reads HT['a0'] -- NOT
//          VT.a0, which appears nowhere in the sibling tree -- and
//          apply_handbook rewrites that to 6.8396 PER RADIAN, so
//          k = a0/(2*pi) = 1.0886/rad and the formula answers
//          3.5004 PER RADIAN -- 51 per cent of the 2-D
//          6.8396/rad, the reduction a fin of AR ~ 1.8 must show, where a
//          per-degree reading would imply about 201/rad.  The trailing pi/180
//          then divides a per-radian number by 180 and stores 0.061093, so
//          every quantity linear in that slope is 57.3x too small and is corrected: CYb,
//          Cnb, Clb and the three Section 7 rate terms that use it.  The evidence that
//          the correction is the right one: AID's Clb is
//          -0.003091, a factor 21.31 below the sibling's own AVL gold Clb =
//          -0.065868 for the same planform (measured), while the corrected -0.068966
//          is within 4.7 per cent of that gold and 1.51x AVL's own
//          alpha = 4 deg row, -0.045756, which avl.jsonc ships.
//      (3) aid/lateral.py:616 returns a HARDCODED 0.1 for both Clda and Cnda and
//          aid/handbook_controls.py has no aileron CY or Cn column at all.  Those
//          0.1 values are a placeholder, not a computation, and are NOT used: cy[1]
//          and cnda[0] are 0.0 and declared in provenance.missing.
//    Every array in this file is per radian and every length is SI.  numpy bridge: numpy.trapezoid present; no alias bound
//    cx[0] -- the parasite drag slot, Cd0 in a drag build-up -- = -0.0172694.
//    Source: aid.drag.aircraft_cd0, which is 1.25 x the summed section drag.  The Fortran's own CD at alpha = 0 is 0.024 with an induced part of 0.00137929,
//    i.e. a parasite of 0.0226207, 1.310x the handbook build-up; both are recorded.
//
// 5. THE MOMENT REFERENCE SHIFT
//    NO MOMENT SHIFT WAS APPLIED, and that is VERIFIED, not assumed: the station
//    is read back out of what the Fortran itself wrote, not out of the same
//    in-memory value it was given.
//    aid/datcom_io.py's write_synths puts XCG={aero['XCG']} on the $SYNTHS namelist, so for005.dat
//    carries $SYNTHS XCG = 2.94 ft and the Fortran ECHOES that namelist back into
//    datcom.out, 2 time(s), all 2.94 ft.  That echo is the number compared against
//    AERO.XCG = 2.94 ft; a disagreement between the card
//    and the echo, an echo that disagrees with itself, or a MISSING echo each raise, so this check
//    cannot pass by comparing AERO.XCG with itself.  Both lists are in provenance.moment_reference.
//    AID's handbook pass takes the same station for the VT arms
//    (hp = VT.Z + VT.ymac - AERO.ZCG, lp = VT.X + VT.xmac + VT.cbar/4 - AERO.XCG).
//    dx = dy = dz = 0, so every moment here is already about the CG and cm[1],
//    cm[0], cm[2], cmq[0], cn[0], cnp[0], cnr[0] are used as they stand.  Contrast
//    tornado.jsonc, whose ref_point is 2.94 ft AHEAD of the CG and is corrected by dx = -0.896112 m.
//
// 6. SIGN CONVENTION
//    cz is the body +z force coefficient with z DOWN, so cz = -CL and cz[1] is NEGATIVE for a
//    stable aircraft (CL_alpha = -cz[1] > 0), and cm[1] is negative too.  That identity is
//    load-bearing; it is what makes data/planes/linear/morelli.json's cz[1] = -4.5 CORRECT, not a
//    defect of that file.
//    data/planes/linear/morelli.json is the sign-convention authority wherever it disagrees with
//    data/planes/f16/morelli.json.  It WINS on cm[1] (-0.8 against +0.0466) and on cnp[0] (-0.03
//    against +0.0268), where a positive value would be statically unstable.  It LOSES on cnda[0]
//    (+0.01 against -0.0335), where adverse yaw fixes the sign.  A reader must not assume the two
//    sets share a convention: this file and linear/morelli.json agree on cz[1] and disagree with
//    the F-16 set on cm[1] and cnp[0].
//    DATCOM, plane/dynamics.py and every in-tree reference file share ONE axis
//    convention: x forward, y right, z DOWN, positive Cm nose up, positive Cl
//    roll right, positive Cn yaw right.  So there is no frame mapping in this
//    file at all.  Which slots, if any, the `cz = -CL` identity still negates is derived:
//    3 negations survive, and none is a frame conversion.  Each is a SIGN-CONVENTION identity that the schema
//    itself requires -- `cz = -CL` on cz[1] and cz[0]; `cx = -CD` on cx[0] -- its body +z axis points down, not a change
//    of axis.  Listed from provenance.mapping:
//      cz[1] from DATCOM for006 CLA at alpha = 4 deg = 0.100300 per degree -> -5.746767 per radian
//      cz[0] from DATCOM for006 CL at alpha = 0 deg = 0.131000 -> -0.131000
//      cx[0] from aid.drag.aircraft_cd0 (via aid.handbook_pass) = 0.017269 -> -0.017269
//    The source planform runs x AFT-positive (XW = 2.2 < XCG = 2.94 < XH = 8.75 ft), which is why
//    tornado.jsonc needs a shift and this file does not.
//    cm0 (Cm0) carries NO invariant -- the two in-tree reference files disagree on it and its
//    sign is tail rigging, not a derivable convention -- so it is written through untouched and is
//    recorded as `not-normalised` in provenance.not_normalised.  Three provenance states exist and
//    all three are recorded: `missing` (field None, slot 0.0), `flipped` (sign normalised against
//    its invariant row) and `not-normalised` (cm0).
//
//    A CAVEAT on the 9 genuinely per-phat / per-qhat / per-rhat slots:
//    cyp[0], cyr[0], czq[0], cmq[0], clp[0], clr[0], cnp[0], cnr[0], cxq[0].
//      NOT rescaled, as in tornado.jsonc. This solver quotes every rate
//      derivative per p*b/(2V), q*cbar/(2V) or r*b/(2V) at the SOURCE
//      Mach-0.03 speed V = 3.1112 m/s, while f16/aero_morelli.py forms phat =
//      p*b_m/(2*V) at the trimmed cruise V = 23.1141 m/s. Restoring the
//      solver's physical dC/dq would mean multiplying the 9 per-hat slots by
//      V_trim/V_source = 7.42933, because czq[0]*cbar/(2*V_trim) must equal
//      CL_q*cbar/(2*V_source); the model's rate-damping terms are that much
//      weaker than the solver implies. Note the plan writes that factor as
//      AS_source/V_trim, which is the INVERSE of the direction that restores
//      dC/dq; nothing is rescaled here either way, so the question is only
//      ever a note.
//    cy[1] and cy[2] are NOT in that list: they are cy_da and cy_dr, the aileron
//    and rudder side force per radian of CONTROL DEFLECTION, which no
//    flight-condition speed touches.
//
//    3 SLOTS ARE GENUINELY ABSENT -- zeroed and declared, with the reason generated from
//    provenance.missing so this list cannot go stale against the slot table below:
//      cnda[0]   cn_da   ZEROED AND DECLARED -- no value exists: aid/handbook_controls.py has no aileron yaw column at all, and aid/lateral.py:616's Clda/Cnda = 0.1 is a hardcoded placeholder, not a computation; using it would put a fabricated number in a coefficient array
//      cxq[0]    cd_q    ZEROED AND DECLARED -- no value exists: DATCOM's Section 7 formulae carry no axial rate derivative at all and aid/longitudinal_dynamic.py hardcodes cxq = 0.0, which records the absence of a formula rather than a computed value
//      cy[1]     cy_da   ZEROED AND DECLARED -- no value exists: aid/handbook_controls.py has no aileron side force column at all, and aid/lateral.py:616's Clda/Cnda = 0.1 is a hardcoded placeholder, not a computation; using it would put a fabricated number in a coefficient array

//    SEPARATELY, 4 SLOT(S) ARE FILLED BUT FLIPPED -- these are NOT absent:
//    they carry a real solver value whose sign the invariant table reversed, so
//    the solver's own sign sits on the other side of zero: cl_da, cl_de, cn_beta, cy_p
//    Every one is in the slot table above with state `flipped`.
//
// 7. PER-SLOT PROVENANCE (all 19 arrays; real solver output vs zeroed)
//   cz[1]     cl_alpha   normalised     solver DATCOM for006 CLA at alpha = 4 deg = 0.1003 -> mapped -5.74677 -> written -5.74677
//   cm[1]     cm_alpha   normalised     solver DATCOM for006 CMA at alpha = 4 deg = -0.01431 -> mapped -0.819903 -> written -0.819903
//   cz[0]     cl0        normalised     solver DATCOM for006 CL at alpha = 0 deg = 0.131 -> mapped -0.131 -> written -0.131
//   cm[0]     cm0        not-normalised solver DATCOM for006 CM at alpha = 0 deg = 0.0472 -> mapped 0.0472 -> written 0.0472
//   cx[0]     cd0        normalised     solver aid.drag.aircraft_cd0 (via aid.handbook_pass) = 0.0172694 -> mapped -0.0172694 -> written -0.0172694
//   cl[0]     cl_beta    normalised     solver aid.lateral.lateral_static.Clb = -0.00309062 -> mapped -0.0689662 -> written -0.0689662
//   cy[0]     cy_beta    normalised     solver aid.lateral.lateral_static.CYb = -0.0093265 -> mapped -0.608244 -> written -0.608244
//   cn[0]     cn_beta    flipped        solver aid.lateral.lateral_static.Cnb = 0.00361948 -> mapped -0.248239 -> written 0.248239
//   czq[0]    cl_q       normalised     NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CL_Q (this converter) -> mapped -5.95022 -> written -5.95022
//   cmq[0]    cm_q       normalised     NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CM_Q (this converter) -> mapped -18.6548 -> written -18.6548
//   cyp[0]    cy_p       flipped        NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CY_P (this converter) -> mapped 0.0353587 -> written -0.0353587
//   cyr[0]    cy_r       normalised     NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CY_R (this converter) -> mapped 0.51156 -> written 0.51156
//   clp[0]    cl_p       normalised     NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CL_P (this converter) -> mapped -0.79861 -> written -0.79861
//   clr[0]    cl_r       normalised     NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CL_R (this converter) -> mapped 0.0953425 -> written 0.0953425
//   cnp[0]    cn_p       normalised     NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CN_P (this converter) -> mapped -0.0344068 -> written -0.0344068
//   cnr[0]    cn_r       normalised     NOT solver output -- DATCOM Section 7 CN_R (this converter) -> mapped -0.287448 -> written -0.287448
//   cxq[0]    cd_q       missing        ABSENT -- DATCOM Section 7 (no axial rate term exists) (see provenance.missing) -> mapped n/a -> written 0.0 (missing)
//   clda[0]   cl_da      flipped        solver aid.handbook_controls[aileron].Cl = 0.00362976 -> mapped 0.20797 -> written -0.20797
//   cz[5]     cl_de      flipped        solver aid.handbook_controls[elevator].CL = 0.00449314 -> mapped 0.257438 -> written -0.257438
//   cm[2]     cm_de      normalised     solver aid.handbook_controls[elevator].Cm = -0.0140867 -> mapped -0.807106 -> written -0.807106
//   cldr[0]   cl_dr      normalised     solver aid.handbook_controls[rudder].Cl = 0.000499984 -> mapped 0.028647 -> written 0.028647
//   cy[2]     cy_dr      normalised     solver aid.handbook_controls[rudder].CY = 0.00454568 -> mapped 0.260448 -> written 0.260448
//   cndr[0]   cn_dr      normalised     solver aid.handbook_controls[rudder].Cn = -0.00191156 -> mapped -0.109524 -> written -0.109524
//   cnda[0]   cn_da      missing        ABSENT -- aid.handbook_controls[aileron].Cn (see provenance.missing) -> mapped n/a -> written 0.0 (missing)
//   cy[1]     cy_da      missing        ABSENT -- aid.handbook_controls[aileron].CY (see provenance.missing) -> mapped n/a -> written 0.0 (missing)
//      cx[1] (CD_alpha) and cx[3] (CD_de) are real schema slots: LINEAR_INDEX
//      lists cx's linear indexes as (0, 1, 3) and f16/aero_morelli.py
//      evaluates Cx0 = cx[0] + cx[1]*alpha + ... + cx[3]*de, so the flight
//      model reads both terms. They ship 0.0 because DerivativeSet has no
//      field for either derivative and aero_convert/morelli.py's SLOT_MAP
//      maps only ('cx', ((0, 'cd0'),)) -- a MAPPING gap, not a schema gap.
//      The linear slots this file leaves at 0.0 without a provenance.missing
//      entry are: cx[1] and cx[3]
//    provenance.zeroed_slots.CD_alpha carries the same fact in full.
//    THE REST OF THIS SOLVER'S RAW, UNCONVERTED OUTPUT IS IN geometry.jsonc, under raw_cross_checks.datcom
//    -- the Fortran's own for006 table and AID's pre-correction handbook rows for
//    DATCOM, the per-alpha .st rows for AVL, the deck and the polar for flow5.
//    A model file's provenance.mapping carries only the one raw number behind each
//    slot, so that block is what makes the rest re-derivable without re-running.
//
// 8. HOW TO REGENERATE THIS FILE
//    cd python && python3 scripts/build_cessna172_aero.py
//    Nothing else writes these files; the sibling analysis and the sibling aid tree
//    are read-only inputs and all solver scratch goes to a temporary directory.
// ==========================================================================
